1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,260
Hey everyone, Josh here.

2
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:05,779
This is the first B three development diary.

3
00:00:05,779 --> 00:00:11,499
I'll share the progress milestone by milestone, including the bugs and the tests

4
00:00:11,499 --> 00:00:12,819
behind each fix.

5
00:00:12,819 --> 00:00:18,538
You're seeing an earlier regtest wallet capture, not a demonstration of production V

6
00:00:18,538 --> 00:00:18,978
two.

7
00:00:18,978 --> 00:00:24,698
Today, I'm looking at the accounting, agreement and restart recovery work behind FlowMesh

8
00:00:24,698 --> 00:00:25,578
V two.

9
00:00:26,978 --> 00:00:28,313
First, the money.

10
00:00:28,313 --> 00:00:34,100
The isolated accounting model follows funds through deposits, shared spot balances, order reservations,

11
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:35,436
fees and payouts.

12
00:00:35,436 --> 00:00:39,443
Two markets must not reserve the same money twice.

13
00:00:39,443 --> 00:00:44,340
Moving cash between spot and futures needs an explicit authorized instruction.

14
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:47,011
Futures cannot silently take spot funds.

15
00:00:47,011 --> 00:00:52,798
These are tested accounting mechanics, not a finished futures trading or margin system.

16
00:00:54,198 --> 00:00:57,697
A balance also needs a believable history.

17
00:00:57,697 --> 00:01:02,694
The model replays its recorded inputs when restoring a snapshot.

18
00:01:02,694 --> 00:01:06,193
A separate checker examines conservation and ownership.

19
00:01:06,193 --> 00:01:12,690
I am keeping the earlier failing examples alongside the repair tests, so the

20
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:14,189
fixes stay inspectable.

21
00:01:14,189 --> 00:01:20,687
But an internally consistent rewritten history still needs an external trusted commitment to

22
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:21,686
detect it.

23
00:01:21,686 --> 00:01:25,684
A passing model is not a security audit.

24
00:01:27,084 --> 00:01:28,137
Next, agreement.

25
00:01:28,137 --> 00:01:34,979
The tests exercise delayed messages, duplicates, missing data, partitions, faulty leaders and late

26
00:01:34,979 --> 00:01:35,506
certificates.

27
00:01:35,506 --> 00:01:42,348
The important question is whether a replica keeps its earlier obligations instead of

28
00:01:42,348 --> 00:01:44,979
forgetting them to make progress.

29
00:01:44,979 --> 00:01:51,295
The checker counts issued synthetic signatures, even ones other replicas never received.

30
00:01:51,295 --> 00:01:55,506
This evidence has fixed membership and synthetic authentication.

31
00:01:55,506 --> 00:01:58,664
It does not qualify live consensus.

32
00:02:00,064 --> 00:02:05,504
Recovery adds actual files, SQLite stores and separate child processes.

33
00:02:05,504 --> 00:02:12,576
Tests interrupt processes at selected boundaries, reopen the stores and check what survived.

34
00:02:12,576 --> 00:02:19,648
A committed accounting batch must not run twice after a restart or duplicate

35
00:02:19,648 --> 00:02:20,192
certificate.

36
00:02:20,192 --> 00:02:23,456
Required signing evidence must remain binding.

37
00:02:23,456 --> 00:02:30,528
These are useful recovery tests, but process kills and injected failures do not

38
00:02:30,528 --> 00:02:34,336
prove safety under actual machine power loss.

39
00:02:35,736 --> 00:02:41,134
For this diary, I repeated four selected isolated suites from one pinned commit.

40
00:02:41,134 --> 00:02:46,531
All three hundred and thirty two tests passed: accounting, agreement, storage and runner

41
00:02:46,531 --> 00:02:46,946
checks.

42
00:02:46,946 --> 00:02:51,098
I'll include the commands and results with the written update.

43
00:02:51,098 --> 00:02:56,496
Native integration, complete futures, bridge qualification and real network latency are separate checkpoints.

44
00:02:56,496 --> 00:03:01,893
That is the progress I can show today, without pretending the whole V

45
00:03:01,893 --> 00:03:03,554
two release is finished.
